Bradley Felton writes:

>    This gulf has to with our differing attitudes toward truth.  Every 
> evidence that I have seen suggests that Extropianism is a form of revealed 
> truth.  The "no debate on the basics" rule that the list members 
> religiously enforce is a sure sign of this.  You know the truth -- it 
> consists of that wisdom made plain in the great books of Heinlein, 
> Friedman, Rand, etc., etc.  Now all you need is a compilation volume that 
> every Extropian could carry around, and maybe an accompanying hymnal...

Another interpretation is that we are self-selecting for a certain set
of axioms we agree on. In my ordinary, non-List life I have always
found kindred spirits who read the same books I read with the same
enthusiasm and with whom I could have interesting debates without
getting bogged down in "debating the basics" with folks who shared
few if any of these core beliefs. This is how we pick our friends.

The List is a cybernetic version of this process.

>    I'm sure that most of you have a hard time buying the concept that 
> Extropianism is a religion, after all, where are the miracles, the burning 
> bushes, parting seas, and all the neat theatrics?  On the other hand, I 
> would wager that most of you don't really believe in the "miracles" anyway.  
> What then is the foundation of a religion, after dispensing with all the 
> glitter and window-dressing:  A group of people with common interests and 
> beliefs who get together and compose a statement of their principles, which 
> they engrave in stone (metaphorically), who then start recruiting other 
> people to join the cause.  As they now possess an agreed-upon standard of 
> truth, they have no interest in debating the basics...  Sound familiar?  
> The problem is, 2000 years later their beliefs have become 2000 years out 
> of date.

There's a difference between a "religion" and a "creed" or a "club." Were the
Founders of this country members of a "Constitution Religion"? Are
hockey players members of a religion (well, a poor example, I guess).

Because we think the ideas of free markets, spontaneous order,
expansion, life extension, privacy, and knowledge are valuable ideas
that should be defended and propagated does not mean we believe in
supernatural occurrences, deities, miracles, prayers, and all the
other trappings of religion.

Ideas have consequences, and we are a voluntary association of folks
who agree on most of a certain core set of ideas. If Bradles mistakes
this for a religion, so be it. This group is clearly not for everyone.

>    The laws of the universe that we inhabit do not allow us to rest 
> certain in our theories, so it behooves a seeker of truth to test the 
> validity of their basic assumptions whenever opportunity permits.  Yet you 
> seem so certain of your "truth" that any dissenting word can be dismissed 
> out of hand...
>  
>    [Descend into metaphor here]  Even and my other beliefs were identical 
> to the Extropian set, still I would not book passage on your ship.  We have 
> both aimed our ships at the future, but in your arrogance, you think you 
> will hit the mark without even looking out the window...  The future is a 
> fleeting target -- I do not believe that any aim could hit the target from 
> this remove.  Frequent course corrections are a must, and I can feel one 
> separating me from you now.  Farewell, and good luck.


And farewell and good luck to you, too, Bradley.

--Tim May


