Subject: May's Social Darwinism

Tim Starr writes to Tim May:
   However, even if you're right that we don't need the masses, it by no
   logical means follows from that that we ought to knowingly alienate them!
   If nothing else, we ought to refrain from making them scared of us, like
   you're so good at doing.  Remember, anything you say can and will be used
   against you.

I felt a responsibility to quote this entire paragraph for the sake of
context.  However, only the middle sentence concerns me:

   If nothing else, we ought to refrain from making them scared of us, like
   you're so good at doing.

I have not yet come up with an entirely satisfactory response to this
in the quarter-hour or so of musing I have done on it, but here is my
attempt at one:

Tim May:  I confirm Tim Starr's contention that you are good at
scaring people.  I don't know if this is your explicit intent; my
impression is that you don't care.  For my own part, this is what
scares me about you, as well as being the only comfort I have.  To
wit:  my impression of the general message that comes across in your
writings is "When you do not amuse me, you are a contemptible bug, and
should count yourself lucky if I decide to ignore you rather than
squashing you like the distasteful annoyance you are.".  And the
"lizard" look you give people comes across (to me, anyway) as "I will
underline that you have no business existing by ignoring you while
looking in your direction.".

Faced with such judgements, I feel uneasy at best (I tried telling
myself I shouldn't, but denying reality didn't get me anywhere).  I
think I may be more prone to such ideas than many, having just
recently cracked the Randian mindset in which (unconditional) love is 
replaced by esteem (read, judgement and assessment).

In short, you petrify me.  I hope that amuses you.

More relevantly to Tim Starr's point, I find the thought of a society
in which all or most people projected the same disdain of and
disregard for humanity that I see in you chilling and depressing, and
suspect that many people who are less sensitized to such "vibes" than
I am would still find that views like yours make them feel uneasy at
best.

Maybe you do it (behave and write the way you do) by design -- maybe
you think it will even help create a world you want to live in.  I
just figured I'd better make sure you were under no delusions that 
there was consensus that it was a Good Thing.

I say what I see -- I don't claim to know how anything, including you,
Really Is.  Perhaps appearances aren't everything, but they aren't
nothing, either.

Kennita "Maybe I'm a person of ideas, but
        they seem to be way different ideas" Watson

