Date: Fri, 24 Dec 1993 15:45:47 -0800
From: kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson)
Subject: May's Social Darwinism

Lest anyone think that I delude myself into believing that I am
discussing ideas, I will point out that my recent posting re my
impressions of Tim has little or nothing to do with his ideas, which
in and of themselves are consonant with my own.  It has to do entirely
with my personal impression of him, and I posted it because I think
that such impressions are at the base of the difficulties that a
number of people seem to be having with him.

I felt that I should make explicit what I was feeling, rather than
couching it in arguments that could not possibly be answered
satisfactorily because they were not rationally based.  "It is useless
to attempt to reason a man out of a thing he was never reasoned into."

Here I sit, musing once again:  Based on the above quote, it is not
only useless to argue with Tim about his methods, but it is useless to
attempt to gather evidence that I should not be afraid of him.  So the
problem (assuming for the moment that considering the fear a problem
is a useful thing to do -- I'm open to another way of looking at it)
can be handled in one of two ways (that I can think of at the moment):

1) Change my perceptions.  This may be involved in the drug odyssey,
   since in my present mindset, I can't even think of how to change it
   or in what direction to create useful results.  There must be
   something I'm missing.  Clearly Tim has friends; what do _they_
   see?  (That's a rhetorical question -- clearly whatever they see 
   is based on interpretation of available information just as
   whatever I see is, and I think their explaining is unlikely to
   attack the interpretational process directly as I think it needs to
   be attacked; thus the drug concept.)
   
2) Think of all the things I think Tim might do to me and make sure he
   can't do them.  There's something to be said for this, but many of
   the defenses would be quite difficult, inconvenient, and
   time-consuming, and many (sometimes the same ones, sometimes not)
   would be repellent to me (I get an image of walling myself inside a
   little bitty "house of bricks", a la Three Little Pigs -- secure,
   but not very comfortable or inviting).

Another bizarre thing I just realized is that I wouldn't fear Tim if I
didn't respect him and admire him in certain ways.  For example, in a
private country, I'd want him defending the borders, because I think
that if he said no enemies would get past him, none would.  Also, I
presume that he is an honest man -- I don't think he makes idle
threats, and if I could reliably sort out his satire from his serious
work, I think I'd find that he believed the things he wrote seriously.
I don't fear people who I think are impotent, or who I think make idle
threats.  Or liars, because I presume people will see them for what
they are.

So -- my feelings about Tim are far from simple, however they may
look from the outside, and pose, at this point Yet Another Damned
Intriguing Psychological Phenomenon (by far my most unsettling
involving other people in recent history).  I'm swamped with same, so
it may be a while before I get around to resolving this one.

Kennita "'People who need people are the luckiest 
        people in the world', my ass!" Watson

