From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Kennita's Emotional Breakdown

Sadly, Kennita Watson has decided the current period of acrimony is
her opportunity to tell the List what she doesn't like about me
personally, how she doesn't like my "lizard gaze" (???) at her, and
how I "petrify" her.

I could simply ignore her overly emotional comments, but instead I
will answer her charges with some facts.

A few months ago, after a nanotech meeting in Palo Alto, we met at a
Lyons restaurant. Dave Krieger, Nick Szabo, Kennita Watson, and I were
talking about various things. Kennita was going on for a long time
about her recovery from Objectivism, about how she was learning she
_was_ a good person, that she _wasn't_ always supposed to be logical
like Spock and Rand, and so on. The three others of us listened as
this went on, very occasionally adding comments of support.

After a while, and in a lull in the conversation, Nick Szabo said to
me: "Tim, you've mentioned Heidegger recently. Could you say more
about him?" So I started to describe his writings.

Kennita interrupted with something along the lines of "Oh, great, now
we have to hear from Tim about more philosophers." (Not having taped
the conversation, I can only approximate what was said. The tone was
disgust that I, or perhaps it was just "the men," would change the
subject from Kennita's Journey to Recovery to something else.)

Feeling we had listened very politely to Kennita pouring out her soul
on Rand and her recovery, and feeling this interruption was very rude
and inconsiderate, I turned to her, looked directly at her (the
"lizard gaze"?) and said something like: "You've had enough time. Nick
has asked about Heidegger, and I'm going to answer him."

I began again to answer Nick's question. Then I heard sobbing. I
looked back over at Kennita, who was on my right (Nick was on my left)
and saw her cradling her head on the table, sobbing and crying. I can
only speak for myself here, but Dave Krieger, Nick Szabo, and I all
looked at each other with the "What now?" puzzlement many men get when
women start crying and bawling.

I suppose the old-fashioned thing to have done was to drop our
conversation, pat her on the shoulder, and say, "There, there,
Kennita, we're sorry we let the conversation change to something
else. You go right ahead and tell us more about your absive mother
reading "Atlas Shrugged" to you as a child. Let it all out. Say what's
_real_ for you. We're here to help you with your recovery."

But, not being old-fashioned, and feeling that women don't need to
coddled and shouldn't be rewarded for manipulative (if it was
that....it could have been just an emotional breakdown)
stereotypically female behavior, we ignored her outburst and attempted
as best we could to continue on--speaking for myself, I was put off by
this bawling in a public place and had a hard time concentrating on
Heidegger. The Heidegger conversation went on for not much longer.

We all studiously and somewhat awkwardly waited until the bawling
stopped. Eventually Kennita recovered, and we all pretty much acted as
if nothing had happened.

However, since that time I have generally avoided talking to Kennita,
having no idea what will trigger another bout of tears. Also, at
various Exi-Bay events, she often reacts to things she hears me say
with a loud "Sigh!" I mean the _verbalized_ form that many computer
people have adopted, not a natural sigh. It's pretty strange to be
saying something at an Extropians and hear Kennita's "Sigh!"
punctuating my remarks. And she sometimes glares at me and announces,
apropos of nothing, "You scare the hell out of me."

Though I've been tempted to reply, "Thank you for sharing that,
Kennita," I instead just ignore these childish and inappropriate
behaviors. I figure it's best to avoid emotionally unstable people.


